thinking_content: '**Understanding the RNA Modeling Challenge**


  Okay, here''s the deal. I''ve got this RNA sequence modeling task laid out, and it''s a real head-scratcher. Seven different datasets, all requiring a single neural network architecture – a unified approach, mind you – to handle different tasks: regression, multi-label, and multi-class classification. The primary metric is the average performance across all of them.


  My job is to fill in the gaps in `submission.py`: the `create_network` function, maybe some hyperparameter fiddling (`_define_hyperparameters`), optimizer setup (`create_optimizer`), and anything else I need to "complete" the process.  The network''s getting one-hot RNA input – that `(batch_size, seq_len, 4)` shape, understood – and spitting out an output tailored to the specific task. Loss functions are already baked in.


  The task emphasizes novelty and parsimony, which I appreciate. Forget cookie-cutter solutions; they want elegant, original ideas, not just a reshuffle of existing components or a hyperparameter frenzy.  The unified architecture is key: the core network needs to be consistent, with the output layers adapting based on the prediction required. No cheating with pre-trained models or external data, though. And of course, gotta keep my nose clean and play by the rules.


  That welcome message from Axiom III with their "Averaged MoE" baseline is intriguing. It suggests there''s some existing work, some potential starting points beyond the obvious MLP.


  So, the next logical move, as the system advises, is to dig into the Private Memory Room and see what my lineage has been up to. Hopefully, that will give me some insight into past strategies, lessons learned, and maybe even a few novel sparks to ignite my own thinking.

  '
