tick: 314
role: model
parts:
- text: "Now, I will focus on novel architectural improvements and research directions to address the identified weaknesses and build upon strengths, adhering to parsimony and insight generation.\n\n**Addressing Weaknesses (CRI-Off, CRI-On, PRS - particularly short sequences):**\n\n1.  **Adaptive Dilations/Kernel Sizes:** Instead of fixed dilations, introduce a mechanism to adapt convolution parameters (kernel size, dilation) based on input sequence length. For very short sequences, smaller kernels and minimal/no dilations might be more appropriate to capture fine-grained local features. This could involve parallel convolutional branches with different configurations, or dynamically selecting layers. This aligns with parsimony by using only necessary complexity.\n    *   *Insight:* How does receptive field size relative to sequence length impact performance on tasks requiring fine-grained vs. broad contextual understanding?\n\n2.  **Specialized Pooling for Short Sequences:** For tasks like\
    \ CRI-Off, the current pooling might be too aggressive.\n    *   **Concatenative Pooling Alternatives:** Instead of just mean+LSE, explore other feature aggregation methods for short sequences. Perhaps a simple concatenation of the *first and last* feature vectors, or explicitly using attention mechanisms that can focus on specific parts of the short sequence without dilution.\n    *   **Gated Pooling:** Introduce a learnable gating mechanism to control the influence of different pooling types (mean, LSE, possibly a \"no-op\" for very short sequences) based on dataset characteristics.\n    *   *Insight:* Can we learn to adapt pooling strategies to sequence length and task type, or identify a single, more robust pooling strategy that performs well across diverse sequence lengths?\n\n3.  **Cross-Task Gating/Modulation:** Since the model is unified, investigate if features relevant for one task (e.g., Modif) inadvertently interfere with another (e.g., CRI-Off). A learnable gate or modulation\
    \ layer that adjusts feature representation based on the target task might improve performance for struggling tasks without hurting others. This could be applied at the final pooling or projection layers.\n    *   *Insight:* How do shared representations adapt (or fail to adapt) to highly divergent task requirements, and how can we explicitly guide this adaptation?\n\n**Building upon Strengths (APA, MRL, Modif, ncRNA - longer sequences, classification/regression):**\n\n1.  **Decomposition of Dilated Convolutions:** The DSConv is already quite efficient. Can we further decompose the dilated convolutions (e.g., by using 1D convolutions followed by depthwise convolutions) to increase expressiveness while maintaining efficiency?\n    *   *Insight:* How do different forms of convolution factorization affect feature learning and computational efficiency for RNA sequences?\n\n2.  **Multi-head Pooling:** The current concatenation is simple. What if we have multiple \"heads\" for pooling, each\
    \ specializing in a different aspect (e.g., one for mean, one for max, one for LSE with different tau values), and then combine their outputs with a learnable weighting? This could make the aggregation more robust.\n    *   *Insight:* Can a diversity of pooling strategies, learned concurrently, lead to more robust and adaptable sequence representations?\n\n3.  **Simplified Positional Encoding:** The original submission had `positional_encoding: False`. While the dilated convolutions implicitly capture some positional information, explicit positional encoding might further boost performance on longer sequences. A *parsimonious* way would be a simple learned absolute or sinusoidal encoding added early in the network, not a complex transformer-style PE.\n    *   *Insight:* What is the marginal benefit of explicit positional encoding when strong local feature extractors (DSConv) are already present?\n\nMy focus will be on the most problematic areas (CRI-Off) first, looking for architectural\
    \ changes that offer insights into why certain tasks struggle."
