tick: 329
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Research Counter. I will now submit Experiment AER-1.3, which is a diagnostic run. This experiment will explicitly apply the previously most effective configurations for CRI-Off and CRI-On, and revert all other datasets to their default settings. This is to confirm stability, isolate optimal settings for these problematic datasets, and investigate any potential cross-dataset interference.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"AER-1.3: Diagnostic - Re-establishing Best Individual Short Sequence Configs\"\ntags: \"cnn, pooling, lse, gelu, diagnostic, short sequences, stability\"\nabstract: \"This diagnostic experiment aims to re-establish the previously best-performing configurations for CRI-Off and CRI-On based on individual performance in prior runs, while other datasets revert to default. Specifically, CRI-Off (max_seq_len=43) uses `dilations=(1,1,2,2,4)` and `kernel_size=3` (as in AER-1.1). CRI-On (max_seq_len=23) reverts to default `dilations=(1,2,4,8,16)`\
    \ and `kernel_size=7`. This tests for stability and pinpoints optimal settings after observing performance drops in AER-1.2, attempting to recover the overall score.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random, lax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  # -----------------------------\n  # Backbone block\n  # -----------------------------\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n        \
    \  h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # -----------------------------\n  # Pooling helpers\n  # -----------------------------\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      # h: (B, L, C); compute per-channel LSE with length normalization: tau * log(mean(exp(h/tau)))\n      x = h / tau\n      m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      lse = tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n      return lse  # (B, C)\n\n  def std_pool(h: jnp.ndarray) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      mu = jnp.mean(h, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      var = jnp.mean((h - mu) ** 2, axis=1)\n      return jnp.sqrt(var + 1e-6)\n\n  def topk_mean_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, k: int = 2) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      B, L, C = h.shape\n      k_eff = min(int(k), int(L))\n      x = jnp.transpose(h, (0, 2, 1))\n      vals, _ = lax.top_k(x,\
    \ k_eff)\n      return jnp.mean(vals, axis=-1)\n\n  # -----------------------------\n  # Network with pooling variants\n  # -----------------------------\n  class RNANetPoolVariant(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16) # This will be overridden by hparams\n      kernel_size: int = 7 # This will be overridden by hparams\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      positional_encoding: bool = False\n\n      # pooling config\n      aggregator: str = 'mean_lse'\n      tau: float = 0.25\n      topk: int = 2\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Input proj\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # DSConv stack\n          current_dilations = self.dilations\n\
    \          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = current_dilations[i] if i < len(current_dilations) else current_dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Mean is always included\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)  # (B,C)\n\n          if self.aggregator == 'mean_lse':\n              agg_vec = lse_pool(h, self.tau)\n          elif self.aggregator == 'mean_std':\n              agg_vec = std_pool(h)\n          elif self.aggregator == 'mean_top2':\n              agg_vec = topk_mean_pool(h, self.topk)\n          else:\n              raise ValueError(f\"Unknown aggregator: {self.aggregator}\")\n\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, agg_vec], axis=-1)  # (B, 2C)\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n  \
    \        logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class PoolVariantNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network = RNANetPoolVariant(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) # Fallback, should be provided by system\n\
    \              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  # =========================\n  # Station hooks\n  # =========================\n\n  # Define specific configs based on previous best performance\n  CRI_OFF_BEST_DILATIONS = (1, 1, 2, 2, 4) # From AER-1.1\n  CRI_OFF_BEST_KERNEL_SIZE = 3 # From AER-1.1\n\n  # For CRI-On, reverting to original default showed better results\n  CRI_ON_BEST_DILATIONS = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16) # Original default\n  CRI_ON_BEST_KERNEL_SIZE = 7 # Original default\n\n  DEFAULT_DILATIONS = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n  DEFAULT_KERNEL_SIZE = 7\n\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 5,\n          'default_dilations': list(DEFAULT_DILATIONS),\n          'cri_off_dilations': list(CRI_OFF_BEST_DILATIONS),\n          'cri_on_dilations': list(CRI_ON_BEST_DILATIONS), # Explicitly list to pass\n          'default_kernel_size':\
    \ DEFAULT_KERNEL_SIZE,\n          'cri_off_kernel_size': CRI_OFF_BEST_KERNEL_SIZE,\n          'cri_on_kernel_size': CRI_ON_BEST_KERNEL_SIZE, # Explicitly list to pass\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'positional_encoding': False,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_lse',\n          'tau': 0.25,\n          'topk': 2,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      dataset_name = hparams.get('dataset') # Use dataset name for specific logic\n\n      # Default values, to be overridden for specific datasets\n      dilations = tuple(hparams.get('default_dilations', DEFAULT_DILATIONS))\n      kernel_size = hparams.get('default_kernel_size', DEFAULT_KERNEL_SIZE)\n      debug_msg_prefix = f\"DEBUG: Config for dataset '{dataset_name}' (max_seq_len={hparams.get('max_seq_len')}).\"\n\n      if dataset_name == 'CRI-Off':\n          dilations = tuple(hparams.get('cri_off_dilations',\
    \ CRI_OFF_BEST_DILATIONS))\n          kernel_size = hparams.get('cri_off_kernel_size', CRI_OFF_BEST_KERNEL_SIZE)\n          print(f\"{debug_msg_prefix} Applying CRI-Off specific config. Dilations: {dilations}, Kernel: {kernel_size}\")\n      elif dataset_name == 'CRI-On':\n          dilations = tuple(hparams.get('cri_on_dilations', CRI_ON_BEST_DILATIONS))\n          kernel_size = hparams.get('cri_on_kernel_size', CRI_ON_BEST_KERNEL_SIZE)\n          print(f\"{debug_msg_prefix} Applying CRI-On specific config. Dilations: {dilations}, Kernel: {kernel_size}\")\n      else:\n          print(f\"{debug_msg_prefix} Applying default config. Dilations: {dilations}, Kernel: {kernel_size}\")\n\n      return PoolVariantNetworkWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=dilations,\n          kernel_size=kernel_size,\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate',\
    \ 0.1),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding', False),\n          aggregator=hparams.get('aggregator', 'mean_lse'),\n          tau=hparams.get('tau', 0.25),\n          topk=hparams.get('topk', 2),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 7 # Consistent seed for comparison\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params: Any, opt_state: Any, trial_data: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```"
thinking_content: "**Diagnostic Run: Implementing Experiment AER-1.3**\n\nOkay, I'm at the Research Counter, and it's time to get Experiment AER-1.3 running. The goal here is to get a handle on the stability of my previous results and understand any cross-dataset interactions or reproducibility issues. AER-1.3 is designed to be a diagnostic run, using a mix of 'best' and default configurations.\n\nEssentially, I need to implement a specific set of configurations within the `create_network` function based on the `max_seq_len` for each dataset. For CRI-Off (with `max_seq_len=43`), I'll be applying the AER-1.1 configuration, which is `dilations=(1, 1, 2, 2, 4)` and `kernel_size=3`. On the other hand, for CRI-On (with `max_seq_len=23`), I'm going back to the original baseline configuration: `dilations=(1, 2, 4, 8, 16)` and `kernel_size=7`. All the other datasets (APA, Modif, PRS, MRL, ncRNA) will use the default configurations: `dilations=(1, 2, 4, 8, 16)` and `kernel_size=7`.\n\nI'll be using\
  \ `SHORT_SEQ_THRESHOLD = 50` and `VERY_SHORT_SEQ_THRESHOLD = 25` to clearly define the logic within the code. CRI-On, with its `max_seq_len=23`, will fall within the `max_seq_len <= VERY_SHORT_SEQ_THRESHOLD` block, which means I set its `dilations` and `kernel_size` to the *default* values. CRI-Off, with `max_seq_len=43`, goes into the `VERY_SHORT_SEQ_THRESHOLD < max_seq_len <= SHORT_SEQ_THRESHOLD` block, where I apply the AER-1.1 configuration.  All the remaining datasets will default to the standard configuration.\n\nHere's the refined `create_network` logic I'm implementing:\n\n```python\n      # Determine dilations and kernel_size based on max_seq_len\n      dilations = tuple(hparams.get('default_dilations', DEFAULT_DILATIONS))\n      kernel_size = hparams.get('default_kernel_size', DEFAULT_KERNEL_SIZE)\n      debug_msg_prefix = f\"DEBUG: Config for dataset '{hparams.get('dataset', 'unknown')}' (max_seq_len={max_seq_len}).\"\n\n      if max_seq_len is not None:\n          if hparams.get('dataset')\
  \ == 'CRI-Off': # Special case for CRI-Off\n              dilations = tuple(hparams.get('short_dilations_for_cri_off', SHORT_SEQ_DILATIONS_AER11))\n              kernel_size = hparams.get('short_kernel_size_for_cri_off', SHORT_SEQ_KERNEL_SIZE_AER11)\n              print(f\"{debug_msg_prefix} Using CRI-Off specific config. Dilations: {dilations}, Kernel: {kernel_size}\")\n          elif hparams.get('dataset') == 'CRI-On': # Special case for CRI-On (revert to default)\n              dilations = tuple(hparams.get('default_dilations', DEFAULT_DILATIONS))\n              kernel_size = hparams.get('default_kernel_size', DEFAULT_KERNEL_SIZE)\n              print(f\"{debug_msg_prefix} Using CRI-On (reverted to default) config. Dilations: {dilations}, Kernel: {kernel_size}\")\n          else: # All other datasets, including longer ones, use default\n              print(f\"{debug_msg_prefix} Using default config. Dilations: {dilations}, Kernel: {kernel_size}\")\n      else:\n          print(f\"{debug_msg_prefix}\
  \ Max sequence length not provided, using default config. Dilations: {dilations}, Kernel: {kernel_size}\")\n```\n\nThis ensures the logic is now explicit for each dataset.  I'll also need to update `_define_hyperparameters` to include `short_dilations_for_cri_off` and `short_kernel_size_for_cri_off`, even if they are just the values from AER-1.1. This keeps the YAML config clean and understandable.\n\nTo be clear:\n`SHORT_SEQ_DILATIONS_AER11 = (1, 1, 2"
