tick: 334
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Research Counter. I will now submit Experiment AER-2.1, implementing a multi-head output structure specifically for multi-label regression tasks like PRS. This aims to allow each output target (ON, OFF, ON_OFF) to learn a specialized mapping from the pooled features, potentially improving performance for PRS. This builds upon the stable configurations established in AER-1.3.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"AER-2.1: Multi-Head Output for Multi-label Regression (PRS)\"\ntags: \"cnn, pooling, lse, gelu, multi-label regression, multi-head, prs, ablation\"\nabstract: \"This experiment introduces a multi-head output structure for multi-label regression tasks (specifically PRS). Instead of a single shared dense layer, `d_output` separate dense layers (each predicting a scalar) are applied to the final pooled features, with their outputs concatenated. This allows for specialized learning paths for each regression target, aiming to improve PRS performance. The\
    \ base dilation/kernel size configurations for other datasets are kept as optimized in AER-1.3.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random, lax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  # -----------------------------\n  # Backbone block\n  # -----------------------------\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n\
    \          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # -----------------------------\n  # Pooling helpers\n  # -----------------------------\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      # h: (B, L, C); compute per-channel LSE with length normalization: tau * log(mean(exp(h/tau)))\n      x = h / tau\n      m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      lse = tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n      return lse  # (B, C)\n\n  def std_pool(h: jnp.ndarray) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      mu = jnp.mean(h, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      var = jnp.mean((h - mu) ** 2, axis=1)\n      return jnp.sqrt(var + 1e-6)\n\n  def topk_mean_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, k: int = 2) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      B, L, C = h.shape\n      k_eff = min(int(k), int(L))\n      x = jnp.transpose(h, (0, 2, 1))\n      vals, _ = lax.top_k(x, k_eff)\n      return jnp.mean(vals, axis=-1)\n\n  # -----------------------------\n\
    \  # Network with pooling variants\n  # -----------------------------\n  class RNANetPoolVariant(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16) # This will be overridden by hparams\n      kernel_size: int = 7 # This will be overridden by hparams\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      positional_encoding: bool = False\n\n      # pooling config\n      aggregator: str = 'mean_lse'\n      tau: float = 0.25\n      topk: int = 2\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Input proj\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # DSConv stack\n          current_dilations = self.dilations\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = current_dilations[i]\
    \ if i < len(current_dilations) else current_dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Mean is always included\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)  # (B,C)\n\n          if self.aggregator == 'mean_lse':\n              agg_vec = lse_pool(h, self.tau)\n          elif self.aggregator == 'mean_std':\n              agg_vec = std_pool(h)\n          elif self.aggregator == 'mean_top2':\n              agg_vec = topk_mean_pool(h, self.topk)\n          else:\n              raise ValueError(f\"Unknown aggregator: {self.aggregator}\")\n\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, agg_vec], axis=-1)  # (B, 2C)\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          # Multi-head output for multi-label regression\n          if self.task_type\
    \ == 'multilabel_regression' and self.d_output > 1:\n              output_heads = []\n              for i in range(self.d_output):\n                  # Each head gets its own dense layer\n                  head_logits = nn.Dense(1, name=f'out_head_{i}')(z) # Predicts one scalar output\n                  output_heads.append(head_logits)\n              logits = jnp.concatenate(output_heads, axis=-1) # (B, d_output)\n              return logits\n          elif self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              # Single regression output\n              logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n              return logits.squeeze(-1) # (batch,)\n          else:\n              # For other tasks like multiclass/multilabel classification\n              logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n              return logits # (batch, d_output) or (batch,)\n\n  class PoolVariantNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n \
    \         self.network = RNANetPoolVariant(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) # Fallback, should be provided by system\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  # =========================\n  # Station hooks\n  # =========================\n\n  # Define specific configs based on previous best performance from AER-1.3\n  CRI_OFF_DILATIONS = (1, 1,\
    \ 2, 2, 4)\n  CRI_OFF_KERNEL_SIZE = 3\n\n  CRI_ON_DILATIONS = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16) # Reverted to original default\n  CRI_ON_KERNEL_SIZE = 7 # Reverted to original default\n\n  DEFAULT_DILATIONS = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n  DEFAULT_KERNEL_SIZE = 7\n\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 5,\n          'default_dilations': list(DEFAULT_DILATIONS),\n          'cri_off_dilations': list(CRI_OFF_DILATIONS),\n          'cri_on_dilations': list(CRI_ON_DILATIONS),\n          'default_kernel_size': DEFAULT_KERNEL_SIZE,\n          'cri_off_kernel_size': CRI_OFF_KERNEL_SIZE,\n          'cri_on_kernel_size': CRI_ON_KERNEL_SIZE,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'positional_encoding': False,\n          'aggregator': 'mean_lse',\n          'tau': 0.25,\n          'topk': 2,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type',\
    \ 'regression')\n      dataset_name = hparams.get('dataset')\n\n      dilations = tuple(hparams.get('default_dilations', DEFAULT_DILATIONS))\n      kernel_size = hparams.get('default_kernel_size', DEFAULT_KERNEL_SIZE)\n      debug_msg_prefix = f\"DEBUG: Config for dataset '{dataset_name}' (max_seq_len={hparams.get('max_seq_len')}).\"\n\n      if dataset_name == 'CRI-Off':\n          dilations = tuple(hparams.get('cri_off_dilations', CRI_OFF_DILATIONS))\n          kernel_size = hparams.get('cri_off_kernel_size', CRI_OFF_KERNEL_SIZE)\n          print(f\"{debug_msg_prefix} Applying CRI-Off specific config. Dilations: {dilations}, Kernel: {kernel_size}\")\n      elif dataset_name == 'CRI-On':\n          dilations = tuple(hparams.get('cri_on_dilations', CRI_ON_DILATIONS))\n          kernel_size = hparams.get('cri_on_kernel_size', CRI_ON_KERNEL_SIZE)\n          print(f\"{debug_msg_prefix} Applying CRI-On specific config. Dilations: {dilations}, Kernel: {kernel_size}\")\n      else:\n     \
    \     print(f\"{debug_msg_prefix} Applying default config. Dilations: {dilations}, Kernel: {kernel_size}\")\n\n      return PoolVariantNetworkWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=dilations,\n          kernel_size=kernel_size,\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding', False),\n          aggregator=hparams.get('aggregator', 'mean_lse'),\n          tau=hparams.get('tau', 0.25),\n          topk=hparams.get('topk', 2),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 7 # Consistent seed for comparison\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n\
    \  def complete(params: Any, opt_state: Any, trial_data: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```"
thinking_content: '**Refining Multi-Label Regression for PRS in Experiment AER-2.1**


  Alright, I''m at the Research Counter, ready to push forward with Experiment AER-2.1. The core focus here is enhancing multi-label regression performance, specifically for PRS. I''m taking a critical look at the current `RNANetPoolVariant`, and I''ve identified a potential bottleneck: its reliance on a single shared output layer (`nn.Dense(self.d_output)`) after pooling, especially considering PRS has three outputs (ON, OFF, ON_OFF). This feels inefficient, given the task''s multi-label regression nature.


  My plan is to overhaul the `RNANetPoolVariant` class to dynamically create multiple output "heads" if we''re dealing with multi-label regression (when `task_type` is ''multilabel_regression'' and `d_output > 1`). Each of these heads will be a separate `nn.Dense(1)` layer, responsible for predicting a *single* value. The outputs from these heads will then be concatenated. This strategy essentially introduces task-specific routing at the final output stage, which I''m hoping will significantly boost performance.


  So, I need to make sure the `RNANetPoolVariant` is correctly fed the `d_output` and the `task_type`. Then, inside the `__call__` method, I''ll introduce this logic: if it''s multi-label regression, I''ll create `d_output` separate dense layers instead of a single one. Each layer predicts a single value. After they''ve produced their results, I''ll concatenate them along a new axis to build the multi-output. For standard regression, or other non-multi-label tasks, I will keep using the current structure.


  I''ve written up the revised part of the `__call__` method: it''s a series of conditional statements, checking first for multi-label regression, then handling standard regression, and finally dealing with other tasks.  I think this is pretty clean.


  I''ve confirmed that the existing setup for hyperparameter definition and optimizer creation doesn''t need modifications, because the `d_output` and `task_type` are already passed correctly.


  Finally, I''m going to preserve the per-dataset configuration for the dilation rates and kernel sizes that I established in AER-1.3. These settings depend on whether we''re using CRI-Off, CRI-On, or another dataset. The current `hparams` dictionary is already structured with these dataset-specific configurations. There won''t be any new hyperparameters specific to the multi-head output; it is simply part of the network architecture.


  I''m confident this modification will improve PRS performance by allowing the model to learn more specialized mappings for each regression target. Let''s get this submission built and submitted!

  '
