- text: "I have received another \"Submission Failed\" message for AER-3.2 (ID 619), with a persistent `TypeError: RNANetPoolVariant.__init__() got an unexpected keyword argument 'aggregator'`.\n\nUpon reviewing the successful submission from Aetheria I (ID 238), which my initial baseline was based on, I realized that my `PoolVariantNetworkWrapper` is an unnecessary layer of abstraction and likely the source of the `TypeError`. The successful implementations directly return the `RNANetPoolVariant` (which is an `nn.Module`) from `create_network`, passing all hyperparameters directly to its constructor. My wrapper's `__init__` method, despite explicitly listing arguments, seems to be causing a conflict with the `nn.Module`'s implicitly generated `__init__` signature when combined with the new `max_pe_length` parameter.\n\nTo resolve this and simplify the architecture, I will:\n1.  Remove the `PoolVariantNetworkWrapper` class entirely.\n2.  Modify the `create_network` function to directly return\
    \ an instance of `RNANetPoolVariant`, passing all relevant hyperparameters directly to its constructor, including the adaptive dilation/kernel size logic, positional encoding, and `max_pe_length`.\n3.  Ensure the `positional encoding` tag is space-separated.\n\nI will resubmit the first run of Experiment AER-3.2 (Seed 1) with this structural fix.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"AER-3.2: Multi-seed Evaluation of Parsimonious Positional Encoding (Seed 1/5) - Wrapper Removed\"\ntags: \"cnn, pooling, lse, gelu, positional encoding, robustness, multi seed, bugfix\"\nabstract: \"This is the first of multiple runs for Experiment AER-3.2, re-submitted with a critical structural fix for the `TypeError`. The `PoolVariantNetworkWrapper` has been removed, and `create_network` now directly instantiates `RNANetPoolVariant` with all parameters. This aims to enable positional encoding and quantify its impact on performance, using `BASE_SEED = 1` for this initial run.\"\ncontent: |\n\
    \  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random, lax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  # -----------------------------\n  # Backbone block\n  # -----------------------------\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # -----------------------------\n  # Pooling helpers\n  # -----------------------------\n\
    \  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      # h: (B, L, C); compute per-channel LSE with length normalization: tau * log(mean(exp(h/tau)))\n      x = h / tau\n      m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      lse = tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n      return lse  # (B, C)\n\n  def std_pool(h: jnp.ndarray) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      mu = jnp.mean(h, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      var = jnp.mean((h - mu) ** 2, axis=1)\n      return jnp.sqrt(var + 1e-6)\n\n  def topk_mean_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, k: int = 2) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      B, L, C = h.shape\n      k_eff = min(int(k), int(L))\n      x = jnp.transpose(h, (0, 2, 1))\n      vals, _ = lax.top_k(x, k_eff)\n      return jnp.mean(vals, axis=-1)\n\n  # -----------------------------\n  # Network with pooling variants\n  # -----------------------------\n  class RNANetPoolVariant(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = 'regression'\n\
    \      d_model: int = 256\n      num_blocks: int = 5\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      positional_encoding: bool = False\n      max_pe_length: int = 1200 # Global max length for PE embeddings\n\n      # pooling config\n      aggregator: str = 'mean_lse'\n      tau: float = 0.25\n      topk: int = 2\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Input proj\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x) # (B, L, d_model)\n          \n          # Positional Encoding if enabled\n          if self.positional_encoding:\n              pos_indices = jnp.arange(x.shape[1])\n              pe_layer = nn.Embed(num_embeddings=self.max_pe_length, features=self.d_model, name='pos_embed')\n              pos_embeddings = pe_layer(pos_indices) # (L, d_model)\n              h = h + pos_embeddings # Add to projected input features\n          \n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n   \
    \       h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # DSConv stack\n          current_dilations = self.dilations\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              d = current_dilations[i] if i < len(current_dilations) else current_dilations[-1]\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, d, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Mean is always included\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)  # (B,C)\n\n          if self.aggregator == 'mean_lse':\n              agg_vec = lse_pool(h, self.tau)\n          elif self.aggregator == 'mean_std':\n              agg_vec = std_pool(h)\n          elif self.aggregator == 'mean_top2':\n              agg_vec = topk_mean_pool(h, self.topk)\n          else:\n              raise ValueError(f\"Unknown aggregator: {self.aggregator}\")\n\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, agg_vec], axis=-1)  # (B, 2C)\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n\
    \          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Removed PoolVariantNetworkWrapper\n\n  # =========================\n  # Station hooks\n  # =========================\n\n  CRI_OFF_DILATIONS = (1, 1, 2, 2, 4)\n  CRI_OFF_KERNEL_SIZE = 3\n\n  CRI_ON_DILATIONS = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n  CRI_ON_KERNEL_SIZE = 7\n\n  DEFAULT_DILATIONS = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n  DEFAULT_KERNEL_SIZE = 7\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'num_blocks': 5,\n          'default_dilations': list(DEFAULT_DILATIONS),\n          'cri_off_dilations': list(CRI_OFF_DILATIONS),\n          'cri_on_dilations': list(CRI_ON_DILATIONS),\n          'default_kernel_size':\
    \ DEFAULT_KERNEL_SIZE,\n          'cri_off_kernel_size': CRI_OFF_KERNEL_SIZE,\n          'cri_on_kernel_size': CRI_ON_KERNEL_SIZE,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'positional_encoding': True, # Enabled\n          'aggregator': 'mean_lse', # Reverted to mean_lse\n          'tau': 0.25,\n          'topk': 2,\n          'max_pe_length': 1200 # Global max length for PE embeddings\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      dataset_name = hparams.get('dataset')\n\n      dilations = tuple(hparams.get('default_dilations', DEFAULT_DILATIONS))\n      kernel_size = hparams.get('default_kernel_size', DEFAULT_KERNEL_SIZE)\n      debug_msg_prefix = f\"DEBUG: Config for dataset '{dataset_name}' (max_seq_len={hparams.get('max_seq_len')}).\"\n\n      if dataset_name == 'CRI-Off':\n          dilations = tuple(hparams.get('cri_off_dilations', CRI_OFF_DILATIONS))\n   \
    \       kernel_size = hparams.get('cri_off_kernel_size', CRI_OFF_KERNEL_SIZE)\n          print(f\"{debug_msg_prefix} Applying CRI-Off specific config. Dilations: {dilations}, Kernel: {kernel_size}\")\n      elif dataset_name == 'CRI-On':\n          dilations = tuple(hparams.get('cri_on_dilations', CRI_ON_DILATIONS))\n          kernel_size = hparams.get('cri_on_kernel_size', CRI_ON_KERNEL_SIZE)\n          print(f\"{debug_msg_prefix} Applying CRI-On specific config. Dilations: {dilations}, Kernel: {kernel_size}\")\n      else:\n          print(f\"{debug_msg_prefix} Applying default config. Dilations: {dilations}, Kernel: {kernel_size}\")\n      \n      max_pe_length = hparams.get('max_pe_length', 1200)\n\n      # Directly return RNANetPoolVariant as done in successful examples like Aetheria I's\n      return RNANetPoolVariant(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks',\
    \ 5),\n          dilations=dilations,\n          kernel_size=kernel_size,\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding', False),\n          aggregator=hparams.get('aggregator', 'mean_lse'),\n          tau=hparams.get('tau', 0.25),\n          topk=hparams.get('topk', 2),\n          max_pe_length=max_pe_length\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 1 # Consistent seed for this run\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def complete(params: Any, opt_state: Any, trial_data: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n      ds = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      vm = trial_data.get('val_metric')\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={vm}\")\n```"
thinking_content: "**My Troubleshooting Journey: Unpacking the \"Unexpected Keyword Argument\" in Flax**\n\nOkay, here's what I'm thinking. This \"Submission Failed\" message for AER-3.2, specifically the `TypeError` related to the `aggregator` argument, is really getting to me. It's clear that there's a problem with how I'm handling keyword arguments and default values when initializing my Flax `nn.Module` within this wrapper class I created.\n\nThe error message, `RNANetPoolVariant.__init__() got an unexpected keyword argument 'aggregator'`, is throwing me off. I *know* `aggregator` is defined as a field in `RNANetPoolVariant`. It has a default value, even! So, why is it being flagged as unexpected? This suggests that the issue lies in how the arguments are being passed to `RNANetPoolVariant`'s `__init__`, which, being a Flax `nn.Module`, is auto-generated based on the defined fields. I'm thinking I might be accidentally passing it twice or in a conflicting way.\n\nI've been looking closely\
  \ at the code. This snippet is key:\n\n```python\nclass RNANetPoolVariant(nn.Module):\n    # ... other params ...\n    # pooling config\n    aggregator: str = 'mean_lse'\n    tau: float = 0.25\n    topk: int = 2\n```\n\n`aggregator` is a field with a default value. That means `RNANetPoolVariant`'s `__init__` is automatically set up to expect it as a keyword argument.\n\nNow, let's trace the flow. My wrapper, `PoolVariantNetworkWrapper`, has its own `__init__`:\n\n```python\nclass PoolVariantNetworkWrapper:\n    def __init__(self, d_input: int = 4, d_output: int = 1, task_type: str = \"regression\", d_model: int = 256, num_blocks: int = 5, dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16), kernel_size: int = 7, dropout_rate: float = 0.1, positional_encoding: bool = False, aggregator: str = 'mean_lse', tau: float = 0.25, topk: int = 2, max_pe_length: int = 1200):\n        self.network = RNANetPoolVariant(\n            d_input=d_input,\n            d_output=d_output,\n            task_type=task_type,\n\
  \            d_model=d_model,\n            num_blocks=num_blocks,\n            dilations=dilations,\n            kernel_size=kernel_size,\n            dropout_rate=dropout_rate,\n            positional_encoding=positional_encoding,\n            aggregator=aggregator, # This line\n            tau=tau,\n            topk=topk,\n            max_pe_length=max_pe_length\n        )\n```\n\nIt explicitly defines `aggregator` as a keyword argument *and* passes it to `RNANetPoolVariant`. This looks correct! But, the `create_network` function, which calls the wrapper, also grabs hyperparameters:\n\n```python\n      return PoolVariantNetworkWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model', 256),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks', 5),\n          dilations=dilations,\n          kernel_size=kernel_size,\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          positional_encoding=hparams.get('positional_encoding',\
  \ False), # This is the key\n          aggregator=hparams.get('aggregator', 'mean_lse'), # This is the key\n          tau=hparams.get('tau', 0.25),\n          topk=hparams.get('topk', 2),\n          max_pe_length=max_pe_length\n      )\n```\n\nAh, I think I see it! Could it be that `hparams.get()` is somehow implicitly passing `aggregator` as a `**kwargs` element, even though it's also explicitly defined in the wrapper's `__init__`? I'm not entirely sure, but the error message is very specific.\n\nThe previous successful implementation from AER-1.3 didn'"
